#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116137} :activity/id #uuid "37fc70ba-9218-5dd8-9943-c92ffa172cf7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048897 :topic/id #uuid "ca2d5c2b-c710-5912-bab0-623e5d6f5c00" :topic/math-academy-id 1731} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116499 :tutorial/content "Just like for $2\\times 2$ matrices, there are also **elementary $3\\times 3$ matrices**. These are matrices that can be obtained by applying *only one* elementary row operation to the identity matrix ![](images/52/5242669707ba340c10907369ae842f98d630582a9e96c9b562eec4e6671d4c6c.svg)\n\nFor instance, consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrices:\n\n![](images/f6/f6a165394b479865919c1a3eff8541ec424fd42e3c487df3d3592f664fe8715c.svg)\n\nThese are all examples of elementary matrices:\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := 2R_2.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + 3R_1.$\n\nThey are also examples of the three different types of elementary matrices, defined by the corresponding row operations." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d18540e-8992-5cf5-9984-837579edd2a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3168 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22f135ef-2bf7-53c7-bbbd-7ceb73df118d" :step/math-academy-id 17234 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116138}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116138 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115252 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115081 :question/id #uuid "083bea4b-54a3-52c9-a1a6-512a9c33d86f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4726"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0303bd06-294e-5f7a-bbd2-b12abdeb93c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050576}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553585}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "80e2ff0b-2184-50d7-9e9f-70274a3019ad" :step/math-academy-id 17235 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115253 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115082 :question/id #uuid "18e5d6e0-a024-503b-87f5-b20080980b51" :question/math-academy-id "e-4727"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41954d0a-b8ce-52a0-a2c0-002b0078a37d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553537}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving an Inverse of an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8f9639d5-a5fb-5b6c-8aca-eb75b34a00ab" :step/math-academy-id 17236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116500 :tutorial/content "As we saw before for elementary $2\\times 2$ matrices, each elementary $3\\times 3$ matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E.$ To illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/a1/a1c71fda15d78cc8c6ae95862c95b73a1cca1830cdf3b54f8e7e151653b82df1.svg)\n\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_3$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/1e/1ef9574c45fb103c7fa2b32af13018de53da495fdf9e6e2da729897a521cead9.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{3}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{3}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1$ transforms $E$ back into the identity matrix. Likewise, when this row operation is applied to the identity matrix, it generates $E^{-1}.$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/fa/fa0e6c42fc8efaf80c3a37640261b1ec8247c2d598f14d7af4865a7c57691654.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $3\\times 3$ matrices in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "90445bd9-d2af-593e-a10d-9e7c94156e49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3169 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e927ae81-0817-515b-8603-18914b2317b0" :step/math-academy-id 17237 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115254 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115083 :question/id #uuid "c27359a0-0784-5750-9323-b35d9d7b5f62" :question/math-academy-id "e-4728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd71abb1-455e-50d0-aae4-082b41821ef1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437049}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "0ad61d7d-e259-50fa-af0b-671e9d0310ef" :step/math-academy-id 17238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116139}}] :activity/title "Elementary 3x3 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115477}]}
